{
  "image_name": "3a53b6a02ccdfb054ae36f1f2886749c.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes ribosome biogenesis and highlights where several inherited disorders disrupt the pathway. Inside the nucleolus, RNA polymerase I transcribes rDNA into a 47S pre-rRNA containing the future 18S, 5.8S, and 28S rRNAs. This precursor is processed and modified, including pseudouridylation, then combined with imported ribosomal proteins and assembly factors to form early pre-40S and pre-60S particles. Separately, RNA polymerase III produces 5S rRNA in the nucleus, which is imported into the nucleolus for large-subunit assembly. Ribosomal proteins and other factors are transcribed by polymerase II, translated in the cytoplasm, and imported back into the nucleus.\n\nThe immature subunits undergo maturation and are exported to the cytoplasm, where the 40S and 60S subunits join on mRNA to support translation; release of eIF6 from the 60S subunit is shown as a late step before productive protein synthesis.\n\nRed lightning symbols mark disease-associated defects at distinct stages: TCS near RNA polymerase I transcription, XL-DC at rRNA pseudouridylation, CHH-AD and DBA during rRNA processing and subunit assembly, and SDS at eIF6 release and late 60S maturation. Overall, the image connects specific ribosomopathies to different steps in making functional ribosomes.",
